Yes. You’re separating knowing, experiencing, and articulating—three things we often compress into “I know.”

Someone can have a strong conceptual model but little lived contact with the phenomenon. Someone else can have decades of experience yet struggle to explain what they know because much of it has become tacit—encoded in perception, timing, intuition, and action rather than propositions. A third person can speak beautifully about something without possessing much of either.

So there are several translations happening:

\text{conceptual knowledge}
\rightleftarrows
\text{lived experience}
\rightleftarrows
\text{tacit knowledge}
\rightleftarrows
\text{articulation}
\rightleftarrows
\text{transfer}

And impostor feelings can emerge in the deltas. You may intellectually understand something but think, Who am I to say this? I haven’t lived enough of it. Or you may have lived it repeatedly but hear someone articulate it with vocabulary you lack and mistakenly conclude that they know something you don’t.

The useful move isn’t choosing theory over experience. It’s repeatedly passing information back and forth across the bridge:

Know something → live it → notice what the model missed → find language for the experience → update the model → live the improved model.

That creates something stronger than either book knowledge or raw experience alone: integrated understanding.

And articulation itself becomes diagnostic. Trying to explain what you “know” exposes holes. Experience then supplies information. Language compresses that information into something transferable. Teaching tests the compression against another mind.

That’s another version of the loop we’ve been building: knowledge gives experience a map; experience corrects the map; articulation makes the corrected map portable.